# Architecture section in PRDs

> **Living rule.** Add new patterns here when the user confirms a new preferred structure in conversation.

## Section placement

Put architecture as **§2a** (between User Stories and Functional Requirements). It is reference material that FRs point into — not a standalone section.

## Structure: one table per comparison, not prose

Use **side-by-side comparison tables** (not separate current-state then future-state blocks):

### Per-flow table (one row per vendor / use case)

Columns: `Flow | Vendor | Current state | Future state | Gap / pain point | Open Qs`

- Keep each cell to 1–3 short sentences.
- Reference open question IDs (e.g. `OQ: G1`) in the Open Qs column — do not repeat the full question.

### Core flow comparison table

For the main availability/reservation flow, use a two-column table:

| | Current state | Future state |
|-|---------------|--------------|

Rows cover the key dimensions: trigger, data source, BOM lookup, JIT, bypass, reservation, reservation ID, part price.

### JIT mechanics table (if JIT is in scope)

Same two-column structure. Rows: orderability signal, job-type exclusions, receiving, scope.

## Architecture decision tagging

- Tag unresolved architecture decisions as **`[ARCH]`** inline wherever they appear (FR table, NFR table, open questions `#` column).
- List all `[ARCH]` items in the open questions table (§7) with Owner and Status.
- `[ARCH]` items **block FR finalization** — call this out explicitly in the PRD footer note.

## FRs referencing architecture

- In the `Open Qs` column of FR table, reference the open question ID (e.g. `A1 [ARCH], D2 [ARCH]`).
- Do not repeat the question text — link to `docs/open-questions.md`.
- When an architecture decision is resolved, remove `[ARCH]` tag from the FR and update the requirement description.
